---
import Base from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import ScopeGate from '../../components/ScopeGate.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Limits and scope"
  description="What postiee can and cannot do, why each omission exists, and the rate limits you will actually hit."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
  <h1>Limits and scope</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    The list of things this can't do is longer than the list of things it can.
    That's deliberate, and every omission has a reason.
  </p>

  <h2>The rate limit</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>150 requests per day, per member.</strong> One post is one request.
    Nothing you do through postiee is pooled with anyone else's — the budget is
    yours alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>search</code> and <code>history</code> are local and cost nothing
    against it. Posting is what spends the budget, and a loop will spend all of
    it in seconds. When you run out, the failure looks like an unrelated error
    rather than a limit, so check this first when a script breaks without a
    clear reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Token life</h2>

  <p>
    Access tokens last <strong>60 days</strong>. Refreshing them automatically
    is reserved for LinkedIn partners, so you re-run <code>auth login</code>.
    If you're still signed in, the consent screen is skipped and it takes
    seconds.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Deleting your own post is not on this list.</strong>
    <code>unpublish</code> deletes it on LinkedIn, using the same self-serve
    <code>w_member_social</code> scope that posting already needs. Only
    <em>reading</em> posts back requires the closed
    <code>r_member_social</code>. See
    <a href="/postiee/docs/commands">Commands</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What postiee can't do</h2>

  <figure>
    <ScopeGate />
    <figcaption>
      Three tiers. postiee operates entirely inside the first.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Comments</td>
          <td><span class="badge mid">partner approval</span> Needs the Community Management API.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reactions and likes</td>
          <td><span class="badge mid">partner approval</span> Same.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Analytics — impressions, followers, profile views</td>
          <td><span class="badge mid">partner approval</span> Separate member analytics scopes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reading your own posts back</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">closed</span> <code>r_member_social</code> isn't accepting requests.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reading who reacted to a post</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">closed</span> Reaction identities are never returned to a member app.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Messaging and DMs</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">no API exists</span> No endpoint in the docs. Mass messaging is an explicit prohibited use.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Connection invitations</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">no API exists</span> Same.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Looking up another member's profile</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">no API exists</span> Person IDs are app-scoped, so there's no way to resolve someone else's.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Running ads</td>
          <td><span class="badge no">separate product</span> The Marketing API is a different platform with its own approval.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scheduled posts</td>
          <td><span class="badge mid">client-side only</span> No <code>publishAt</code> field exists. Scheduling means a local timer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Multi-image and video posts</td>
          <td><span class="badge mid">partner approval</span> One image works self-serve. <code>post --image</code> takes exactly one, and there is no flag for more.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why so many omissions</h2>

  <p>
    LinkedIn's API is not built for individuals posting to their own feed. It is
    built for marketing platforms managing thousands of company pages. So the
    surface that would actually help you — reading your posts, reacting to them,
    seeing how they performed — is exactly the surface that requires becoming a
    vetted partner.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <code>comment</code> command that 403s on first use teaches you nothing and
    wastes your afternoon. An absent command tells you the truth immediately. So
    the tool ships only what works, and this page says why the rest is out of
    reach.
  </p>

  <div class="note info">
    <p>
      <strong>If LinkedIn ever opens one of these</strong>, the architecture will
      take it. The local history already stores what a comments table would need
      — the post URN is the join key.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is guaranteed to keep working</h2>

  <p>
    These are pinned by tests in the repository, so a change that breaks one
    fails the build rather than quietly changing behaviour.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Porter stemming — <code>authentic</code> finds <code>authentication</code></td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Multi-term AND narrows rather than widens</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>An absent term returns nothing</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Junk input never errors — <code>"</code>, <code>AND</code>, <code>()</code>, <code>NEAR(a b)</code>, <code>--</code></td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Re-recording a post upserts instead of duplicating</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deleting removes the search index entry too</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deleting on LinkedIn sends <code>X-RestLi-Method: DELETE</code></td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deleting an already-deleted post is accepted, not an error</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Post ID read from the response header, not the body</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Image upload performs exactly two calls</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>URNs in paths are fully percent-encoded</td><td><span class="badge ok">pass</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Run <code>go test ./...</code> to confirm them yourself.
  </p>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Partial-word prefix search does not work, and is not going
      to.</strong> SQLite FTS5 indexes whole tokens, so
      <code>search harn</code> misses <code>harness</code>. It is called out here
      because a limit you know about is cheaper than one you find at 2am — not
      because it is queued for support.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>